window.PARTS = [{"p": 1, "cls": "3.1", "title": "Building the Training Page", "first": 1, "last": 50, "count": 50}, {"p": 2, "cls": "3.2", "title": "Reading What the Detector Produces", "first": 52, "last": 101, "count": 50}];
window.SLIDES = [{"n": 1, "part": 1, "title": "Building the Training Page", "paras": ["Good evening, everyone. Let's wait five (5) minutes before we start the class, so everyone can connect… All right, today we start Module three (3). Here, your detector is going to learn to find your own objects. We'll start building the training page. And on Wednesday, we're going to read and understand the results. This week Claude won't only help you write code: it's also going to question your ideas and review your results. Just so you know, at the end of the class I'll stay with a student who needs help setting up their laptop. If anyone else needs help, you can stay; I'll make the adjustments in private. If you don't have any problems, you can disconnect when we finish."]}, {"n": 2, "part": 1, "title": "Last Week: Your Data Page", "paras": ["Before we start, let's remember where we stopped last week.", "1. First, you built the first page of your system, the data page.", "2. Second, you observed your data in detail before trusting it. And you saw something that will matter a lot today, that many of the boxes are very small.", "3. Third, your first module, cnn.py, which you used with photos from your field.", "4. Well, today we take the big step. Until now, the model knew nothing about your objects. Today it starts to learn them, and your system gets its second page."]}, {"n": 3, "part": 1, "title": "Where We Are", "paras": ["Ok, let's find where we are on the course map. We already finished the modules in gray, and now we're in Module three (3), in red.", "1. Today, in Class three point one (3.1), we're going to see what a training really does, and what decisions change everything. And at the end we build the page with Claude.", "2. And on Wednesday, in Class three point two (3.2), we read the results. In other words, what do those numbers mean? And can we trust them? There you'll meet your third Claude Code tool, the subagents."]}, {"n": 4, "part": 1, "title": "What Training Really Does", "paras": ["Well, let's start. Everybody talks about training a model. But what really happens when we press the train button?"]}, {"n": 5, "part": 1, "title": "One Batch, One Epoch", "paras": ["Let's see it step by step. I won't use formulas, so you can understand it more clearly.", "1. First, we have all your training photos.", "2. Second, the network doesn't look at them all at once, it looks at them in small groups, and each small group is called a batch. And why in small groups? Because a computer can't look at hundreds of photos at once, it runs out of memory. And also, adjusting the numbers a little many times works better than adjusting them only once with everything.", "3. Third, with each batch it does the cycle we saw in Module two (2). It guesses, it compares, and it adjusts its numbers a little. It's exactly what we saw last week. The network gives an answer, we measure how far it was, and each number of the filter moves a little in the direction that improves it.", "4. And when all the photos have gone through, that's one epoch. You're going to see that word everywhere. When someone tells you they trained for thirty (30) epochs, it means the network saw all the photos thirty (30) times.", "5. And that repeats many times. That's why training isn't magic: it's a huge number of small adjustments, one after the other."]}, {"n": 6, "part": 1, "title": "Watching It Learn", "paras": ["Now let's see how that looks in a real training, from the project I prepared for the course.", "1. Look at how it grows. On the left, the loss, which tells us how wrong the network is, goes down. The dark line is the training photos, and the red one is photos the network doesn't use for learning. On the right, how well it detects, and it goes up. And why do we care about the red line? Think of it as if the dark one were the homework it already did at home, and the red one were an exam with new questions. What we want to know is whether it really learned, not whether it remembers.", "2. And look at the shape. At the beginning it improves fast, and at the end it almost doesn't change. This means that most of the learning happens early, and that will help us decide how many epochs are worth training. Think about it for your project. If after a certain point it almost doesn't improve, training twice as long doesn't give you twice as much. That's your computer's time, and above all your time."]}, {"n": 7, "part": 1, "title": "Three Questions Inside the Loss", "paras": ["Open your results file and you'll find three (3) losses. Three numbers that should go down. But what is each one measuring?", "1. The first one asks a simple question: is the box in the right place? Saying \"there's a pothole\" isn't enough. The box has to be where the pothole is.", "2. The second one asks: is it the right object? And if you only have one class, the question becomes even simpler: is there an object here, or nothing at all?", "3. And the third one is about precision. How exact are the corners of the box?", "4. You don't need to memorize them. Here's what matters: they should go down, and the validation one should go down with them. If it doesn't make sense to you, that's what Claude is for, as you'll see in a minute. And one warning sign: if the training loss goes down but the validation loss goes up, something is wrong. We'll come back to that very soon."]}, {"n": 8, "part": 1, "title": "Ask Claude to Read Your Run", "paras": ["Reading those curves used to take a lot of experience. So here's a question: what if someone could read them with you?", "1. This week your kit has a new Skill, called explain-run. Remember last week's Skills? A Skill is a file with written steps. This one reads your results file and explains it in simple words. So I asked it a simple question: how did my training go?", "2. And here's its real answer. First, the facts: how long it took, and which epoch was the best. Then something I didn't expect. The model practically stopped improving halfway through. And it warned me about a sign I had missed: the class loss on validation stopped going down. But the best part is the last line. It asks me: what would you have to compare this result with, to know that using all the photos was the best choice? That's a great question. One training, on its own, proves nothing. To know if something is better, you need something to compare it with.", "3. So the Skill reads the numbers for you. But it doesn't write the conclusion. The conclusion is still YOURS."]}, {"n": 9, "part": 1, "title": "best.pt and last.pt", "paras": ["The training is over. So what's left on your computer?", "1. A folder like this one. Ultralytics creates it by itself, with the results and the weights of the model. And results.csv is the same file Claude just read.", "2. Inside, two files that people often mix up. best.pt is the epoch that did best on validation. That's the one you use. last.pt is simply the last epoch. It may be worse, but it lets you continue a training later. Wait: how can the last epoch be worse than an earlier one? Because, as you're about to see, a network can start to memorize. That's exactly why Ultralytics keeps the best one separately.", "3. And remember last week: these files are heavy. They stay on your computer. They never go to GitHub."]}, {"n": 10, "part": 1, "title": "When Training Goes Too Far: Overfitting", "paras": ["Now, a famous problem. Can you train too much?", "1. To make it easy to see, I trained the model with very few photos, only thirty (30). Look at the two curves.", "2. This has a name: overfitting. The network memorizes its photos instead of learning the object. Think of a student who memorizes the answers of the practice exam. Perfect on the practice. Terrible on the real exam. So how do we catch it? With the two (2) lines. That's the reason we keep photos the network never learns from. If we only looked at the training line, everything would look perfect.", "3. Look at what happens. The dark line keeps going down. The red one stays up. The network keeps getting better on its own photos, and stops getting better on new ones. With so few photos it reaches zero point five (0.50) on the exam. With all of them, zero point eight two (0.82). One warning: the overfitting message on the page is a hint, not a proof. That's why you look at the curves yourself. And the cure is almost never \"train less\". It's \"get more photos\"."]}, {"n": 11, "part": 1, "title": "Transfer Learning", "paras": ["You know what a training does. Now, where does it start? Because that one decision changes almost everything."]}, {"n": 12, "part": 1, "title": "From Zero, or From COCO?", "paras": ["In Module two (2) I told you we wouldn't train from zero. But don't take my word for it. I measured it.", "1. Same network. Same photos. Same number of epochs. Trained two (2) times. The only difference is the starting point. One starts from zero: its numbers begin at random, as if it had never seen anything. The other starts from the COCO weights: it has already seen hundreds of thousands of photos, so it already knows how to see edges and shapes. Look at the two curves.", "2. From zero, it reaches zero point three eight (0.38). From COCO, zero point eight two (0.82). More than double, for the same work. And the red curve was still climbing, so with many more hours it would get better. But who has that many hours?", "3. Same network, same computer time. The only thing that changed was where it started. That's how much the starting point matters."]}, {"n": 13, "part": 1, "title": "What Transfer Learning Assumes", "paras": ["But transfer learning has a hidden assumption. What is it?", "1. Let's start with the easy case. Your photos look like normal COCO photos: a street, taken by a person. Then it works very well. That's why our potholes work so well.", "2. Now a satellite photo. Everything is seen from above, so objects look completely different. It still helps, but less.", "3. And an X-ray. It looks nothing like COCO. Here you have to check carefully. That doesn't mean it fails. It means you can't take it for granted.", "4. So here's the assumption: what the network already knows how to see is useful for your photos. And you don't assume it. You test it. If your field is very different, that's a perfect question for your experiment."]}, {"n": 14, "part": 1, "title": "What Stays and What Changes", "paras": ["So when we start from COCO, what stays inside the network, and what changes?", "1. First, the backbone. It already knows how to see edges and shapes. It stays.", "2. Second, the neck. It mixes the different sizes. It stays too.", "3. Third, the head. This is the part that says what the object is and where it is. And this one has to change. Why? Because COCO has eighty (80) classes, and none of them is \"pothole\". So the head is replaced by one with exactly your classes, the ones you wrote in project.yaml.", "4. And the good news: you don't do any of this by hand. Ultralytics does it for you. Then the training adjusts the whole network a little, with your photos."]}, {"n": 15, "part": 1, "title": "Let Claude Design the Comparison", "paras": ["So how would you test this in your own project, without fooling yourself? Let's ask Claude.", "1. Here's the request. I ask it to design the fairest comparison I can run on my machine, and not to train anything yet. Look at how it's written. I'm not asking it to train. I'm asking it to think with me. And I tell it not to touch anything.", "2. And here's what it proposes. Change only one thing, the starting weights, and keep everything else the same. Measure only with the test photos. And it adds a warning: with one seed and few photos, a small difference proves nothing. That's not writing code. That's using AI to think better about the experiment."]}, {"n": 16, "part": 1, "title": "What Do You Train On?", "paras": ["And now, the decision almost nobody talks about. Before you train: which photos will you train on?"]}, {"n": 17, "part": 1, "title": "Three Ways to Choose", "paras": ["There are three (3) paths.", "1. First, train on everything. That's what almost everybody does, without thinking about it. And often it's the best option. But it should be a decision, not an accident.", "2. Second, just one class. If you only care about STOP signs, why train on the others? It sounds logical. The network focuses on one single thing. But is that true? We'll find out.", "3. Third, a filtered subset. For example, only the photos where the object looks big, because you think small objects confuse the model. A reasonable idea. And we'll test it too.", "4. Here's the key point: this choice isn't a detail you make before the experiment. It IS part of the experiment. That's why the page you build today lets you choose."]}, {"n": 18, "part": 1, "title": "The Choice Changes the Result", "paras": ["So what happens with real numbers, on our potholes?", "1. I changed only the training photos. The test photos stayed exactly the same. With all the photos: zero point eight two (0.82). With only the large potholes: zero point seven one (0.71). It went down.", "2. Why? Because the exam didn't change. If you train only on large potholes, the small ones are still there on exam day. And in the real world it's the same. Your users won't send your detector only the easy photos.", "3. So the photos you train on decide what your detector is ready for."]}, {"n": 19, "part": 1, "title": "One Class, or All of Them?", "paras": ["Now the other idea, with the traffic signs. Is one specialist better than a generalist?", "1. I compared two (2) models on the same STOP signs, with the same test photos. One learned all four (4) classes. The other learned only STOP.", "2. And this surprised me. The specialist didn't win. Both score very high, and the one with four (4) classes is even a little higher. Careful, though: there are only fourteen (14) STOP signs in the test, so that small difference doesn't mean much. What we can say is this: specializing didn't help. Why not? One possibility: with the other classes, it has more photos to learn edges and shapes. I don't know for sure. And that's what makes it a great question for your own experiment."]}, {"n": 20, "part": 1, "title": "Write Why, Before You Train", "paras": ["And now the most important rule of this part. Before you train, write down why.", "1. Here's an example, in the file docs/experiment.md that comes in your kit. It sounds very reasonable: small potholes are hard, so I'll train only on the large ones, and it will improve.", "2. And YOU write this file, before training. Why before? Because if you don't write what you expect, any number will look good to you. Writing it first is the only way to know, later, whether you learned something or just got a number. And don't worry about being wrong. The grade doesn't depend on being right. It depends on saying what you expected, and explaining what happened. A scientist who's always right probably isn't writing anything down first."]}, {"n": 21, "part": 1, "title": "Claude Attacks Your Hypothesis", "paras": ["Now, what if we ask Claude to try to break that idea, before we spend an hour training?", "1. Here's the request. Look at the last two (2) sentences: use the real counts, and don't write my reason. Those two sentences make all the difference. Without the first one, you get general advice. Without the second one, Claude writes your reason, and then it isn't yours anymore.", "2. And here's the answer. I loved this. It went to my data and told me something simple: the filter only changes the training; the exam stays the same. And half of the test boxes are small. Then, instead of giving me a conclusion, it asked me three (3) very uncomfortable questions. I had written my hypothesis thinking it was very good. One answer, and I realized I had never looked at the exam.", "3. And that's the point. It didn't write my reason. It made me think better. That's how we want to use AI in this course: not so it thinks for us, but so it makes us think better."]}, {"n": 22, "part": 1, "title": "What Really Happened", "paras": ["So who was right? I trained it, to find out.", "1. Here are the two (2) models, with the same test photos.", "2. My prediction said it would go up a lot. It went down, from zero point eight two (0.82) to zero point seven one (0.71), and it found fewer potholes. Claude's questions were right. And this isn't a failure. This is exactly what an experiment teaches you. If I hadn't written my prediction first, I might have invented an excuse."]}, {"n": 23, "part": 1, "title": "Claude Suggests the Next Run", "paras": ["You have a result. Now the real question: what next?", "1. I asked Claude for the next experiment, with one condition: change the data, not the model. And I asked for something almost nobody asks: what result would prove it wrong?", "2. Here's what it found. Something I had completely missed. My filter removed a quarter of the photos, but almost half of the boxes. So what hurt the result: fewer photos, or fewer small boxes? I couldn't tell. So it proposed training with the same number of photos, chosen at random. And it bet on a number. It was so easy to test that I ran it. Zero point seven nine (0.79). Claude won its bet. The main cause seems to be the size of the boxes, although it's only one run. That took a couple of minutes. And the decision to run it was still mine."]}, {"n": 24, "part": 1, "title": "Your Experiment on One Page", "paras": ["To sum up this part: your whole experiment fits on one page.", "1. First, before training, you write which data you chose, why, and what you expect. Three (3) sentences. Not an essay.", "2. Second, Claude questions it, with your real counts, and without writing it for you.", "3. Third, after training, you write what really happened, and what you learned. This is where the learning happens: comparing what you expected with what came out.", "4. And this page goes in your repository, because it's part of Assignment three point one (3.1)."]}, {"n": 25, "part": 1, "title": "Break 1 · 5 Minutes", "paras": ["Well, this is the end of the first part. You now know what a training does, why we start from COCO, and why the photos you choose are part of the experiment.", "1. Now we're going to take a five (5) minute break, while I turn off my camera and microphone. On the screen you'll have a timer, so you know when we start the class again.", "Welcome back, everyone. Let's continue with the second part: now I want us to build the page."]}, {"n": 26, "part": 1, "title": "Four Settings, and Why They Matter", "paras": ["Before we build it, four (4) words you'll see on the page.", "1. The epochs: how many times it sees all the photos. The image size: remember, it matters for small objects. The batch: how many photos in each step. And the seed: so you can repeat the result. Here are the values we use, and you can start with those. If that sounds like a lot, don't worry: the page already comes with them. And if your object is very small, the image size is the first thing to try. Remember the street photo from last week?", "2. And one very important tip: change one thing at a time. Change three (3) things, and if the result improves, you won't know which one did it."]}, {"n": 27, "part": 1, "title": "How Long Will It Take?", "paras": ["And the question everybody asks: how long will it take? Let's ask Claude.", "1. I gave it the results of a short test, two (2) epochs, and asked how long thirty (30) epochs with all the photos would take. And the request says clearly: don't start training. If you don't say it, it might.", "2. Here's the answer. At that speed, almost five (5) hours. But then it adds: probably a little more than one (1) hour, because another training was running on the machine at the same time. And it was right. My computer was busy. So it didn't just do the math. It told me why the math could be wrong.", "3. So here's the tip. Train two (2) epochs first. You confirm everything works, and Claude does the math before you give it your whole evening."]}, {"n": 28, "part": 1, "title": "Let the Page Train in the Background", "paras": ["Here's a question that will help you read Claude's plan. Why doesn't the page freeze while it trains?", "1. First, you press the button on the page.", "2. Second, the page doesn't do the training itself. It starts a separate process, and that process does the work. It's like sending a long document to the printer: the computer doesn't freeze while it prints. You keep working.", "3. Third, every time an epoch finishes, that process writes a new line in results.csv.", "4. Fourth, every few seconds, the page reads that file and draws the progress.", "5. Now imagine the training ran inside the page. The page would freeze until the end. That's why the specification asks for a separate process. So when you read Claude's plan, you know what to look for. If it doesn't mention a separate process, ask."]}, {"n": 29, "part": 1, "title": "No Powerful Computer? Plan B", "paras": ["And what if your computer can't handle it?", "1. First, your own computer is the normal path. I tried it on a computer with no graphics card: about an hour and twenty minutes. Start it, and go have dinner. You don't need a powerful computer for this course. You need patience.", "2. Second, if it really can't, Google Colab lends you a graphics card for free, for a while. You train there, download best.pt, and bring it back to your project. The guide has the steps.", "3. Either way, the page and the code stay in your repository."]}, {"n": 30, "part": 1, "title": "Now We Build the Page", "paras": ["Now we're really going to build the page. The same way as in Module two (2): with Claude Code."]}, {"n": 31, "part": 1, "title": "The Specification", "paras": ["It all starts with the specification. It's in your kit, in the file docs/train_spec.md.", "1. Here's what it asks for. Choose the data without touching the original files. Train in a separate process. Read the progress, and warn if there's overfitting. And the page: you choose, you say why, you start, and you watch. The specification says WHAT each function must do, not HOW to write it. The how is Claude's job. And \"without touching the originals\" matters: you know how much work it took to prepare that data.", "2. And it comes with ten (10) tests that run in seconds, without training anything. Why tests that don't train? Because if each test took an hour, nobody would run them. These use tiny made-up data, and check the logic in seconds. The real training comes after they pass."]}, {"n": 32, "part": 1, "title": "The Request", "paras": ["Here's the request.", "1. I ask it to read CLAUDE.md and the specification, and to give me a plan first, with how it will check each part. No code yet. And one more question at the end: what do you need from me? That question is very useful, because there are often things Claude can't decide alone.", "2. It's the same method as always: specification, plan, approval, tests, commit. But this time, something happened that I didn't expect."]}, {"n": 33, "part": 1, "title": "Claude Found Mistakes in My Spec", "paras": ["And this really surprised me.", "1. Claude read my specification, and found mistakes in it. The first one: the specification said one thing, and the tests said another. The second one is more serious. No test caught it, and it would have broken the metrics of anyone training with one single class. Let me explain it slowly. If you train with one class and change its number, but the exam still uses the old labels, then the model and the exam aren't talking about the same thing. The metrics come out wrong, and nobody notices.", "2. And remember: I wrote that specification, and I had already tested it. Both problems were real. I fixed them. So the plan is not a formality. Read it. It can teach you something. Claude isn't only the one who writes. It's also the one who reviews what you wrote. That's really using AI."]}, {"n": 34, "part": 1, "title": "Approve, Build, Test", "paras": ["I approved the plan, and Claude did its work.", "1. Here's the real output. The ten (10) new tests pass. And the tests from the earlier modules still pass. That second line matters a lot. Adding something new must not break what you already had. That's what the old tests are for.", "2. It took about six (6) minutes. And at the end it told me something very valuable: what it did NOT check. \"I haven't seen this in a browser, and I haven't done a real training.\" Why did it say that? Because CLAUDE.md asks it to show the real output.", "3. Keep that in mind. An honest \"I didn't check it\" is worth much more than \"it works\"."]}, {"n": 35, "part": 1, "title": "The Page, Running", "paras": ["And here's the result, with a real training run.", "1. At the top, you choose the data, and it tells you how many photos that is. Then you write why, and you start. Below, the progress, epoch by epoch, in a table. Look at the box that asks why you chose that data. We asked for that box on purpose, in the specification. The system itself makes you think before you train.", "2. And meanwhile, the page keeps responding. This is the second page of your system."]}, {"n": 36, "part": 1, "title": "Then I Checked It Myself", "paras": ["Claude told me what it hadn't checked. So I checked it myself.", "1. I opened the page: both tabs are there. I chose half of the photos: it showed the right number. I checked that it saved my reason in a file. I started a real training: Ultralytics accepted the data. And I watched the progress go up. And then something interesting. After only two (2) epochs, the program already said there was overfitting. With so few epochs, that means nothing. So I added that rule to the specification. You only find that kind of thing by using it.", "2. Here's what I want you to remember. The tests check the code. Only you can check that it does what you wanted."]}, {"n": 37, "part": 1, "title": "And If You Just Say “Add a Page”?", "paras": ["What if you skip the specification, and just say: \"add a training page\"?", "1. I tried it, so you could see.", "2. Good news first: it didn't write code, because your CLAUDE.md tells it to propose a plan first. But look at the plan. It decides the design by itself: buttons I didn't ask for, a list of runs. And it plans to write its own tests. In other words, it writes its own exam. It's not wrong. But those are its decisions, not yours. That's why we write the specification first."]}, {"n": 38, "part": 1, "title": "When It Is Not What You Asked For", "paras": ["Sooner or later, Claude will give you something that isn't what you asked for. So what do you do?", "1. First, stop, and say what's wrong, in concrete words. Not \"it doesn't work\". Say \"the page freezes while it trains\". Claude can't see your screen. The more concrete you are, the better it understands.", "2. Second, paste the evidence: the error, the screenshot, the output.", "3. Third, ask for one single change, and tell it not to touch the tests. Ask for ten (10) things at once, and if something breaks, you won't know which one did it.", "4. And fourth, run it again yourself, and write it in LOG.md. Remember: Assignment three point one (3.1) asks for your requests, including the ones that didn't work. So don't delete them. They count too."]}, {"n": 39, "part": 1, "title": "A Small Commit, Then a Real Run", "paras": ["Before the real training, two (2) things.", "1. First, a small commit. Check with git status that no models and no results are going to GitHub. Then, for the first time, a run of two (2) epochs, from start to end. And only then, your real training. Two (2) epochs tell you that the data is read, the model saves its files, and the page shows the progress. They don't tell you how good it is. That doesn't matter yet.", "2. Why bother? Because a mistake found at two (2) epochs costs you minutes. Found at thirty (30), it costs you the night."]}, {"n": 40, "part": 1, "title": "Two Detectors, the Same Data", "paras": ["So far we've talked about YOLO. But in Module two (2) we met two (2) detectors. What happens when we give them the same data?"]}, {"n": 41, "part": 1, "title": "The Same Photos, Two Runs", "paras": ["The syllabus asks us to compare them. So how do we do it fairly?", "1. You train YOLO. I trained Faster R-CNN, to show you, because on a normal computer each epoch takes much longer. And that's why they don't have the same number of epochs. When you compare them, you have to say that too.", "2. And here's the part that matters most: the same training photos, and the same test photos. Without that, the comparison means nothing."]}, {"n": 42, "part": 1, "title": "Watching Them Differ", "paras": ["And here are the results.", "1. On the left, how well each one detects. On the right, how long each one takes per photo. In our project, YOLO wins both. Remember Module two (2): Faster R-CNN works in two (2) stages. That's why it's slower. You might expect that extra time to make it more accurate. It didn't, here. That's what measuring is for.", "2. But careful. That doesn't mean YOLO always wins. With other data, or another training, it could change. That's why we measure instead of guessing."]}, {"n": 43, "part": 1, "title": "Ask Claude to Compare Them", "paras": ["And, as usual this week, we can ask Claude.", "1. I gave it the results file and asked three (3) things: which one is more accurate, which one is faster, and which comparison wouldn't be fair.", "2. And look at that last part. It says it isn't fair to compare them at the same confidence threshold, because a score from one doesn't mean the same as a score from the other. That's a very common mistake. I could have made it myself."]}, {"n": 44, "part": 1, "title": "Which One Would You Keep?", "paras": ["So, which one would you keep? It depends on one question.", "1. First, an app on a phone, on the road. Speed and a small model matter. If it takes too long, the car has already driven over the pothole.", "2. Second, a report for the city, running at night. Time doesn't matter so much. Being right does.", "3. And third, your own project. Ask yourself: what does your user lose if it's slow, and what do they lose if it's wrong? There's no best detector in general. There's a best detector for your problem."]}, {"n": 45, "part": 1, "title": "Activity, Part 1: Build It and Train It", "paras": ["Now it's your turn. This is the first part of the activity, in five (5) steps.", "1. First, fill in docs/experiment.md: your data, why, and what you expect. You write it yourself.", "2. Second, ask Claude to question it, with your real counts.", "3. Third, build src/train.py and the page. Plan, approval, and the ten (10) tests.", "4. Fourth, run two (2) epochs from the page, and then your real training.", "5. And last, ask explain-run how it went, and write the \"after\" part yourself. IMPORTANT: with this, you already have almost all of Assignment three point one (3.1)."]}, {"n": 46, "part": 1, "title": "Your Week", "paras": ["That's the concept part of today. What's left is for your week: the most common mistakes, Assignment three point one (3.1), and how to document your work."]}, {"n": 47, "part": 1, "title": "Common Mistakes and How to Spot Them", "paras": ["Before the assignment, the four (4) mistakes I expect to see the most.", "1. First, a training that never ends, because you started it at full size. Run two (2) epochs first, and ask for an estimate.", "2. Second, \"it finished, so it worked\". Look at the curves. Did it really improve on validation?", "3. Third, using last.pt. Use best.pt, unless you have a reason.", "4. And fourth, measuring with training photos. The only exam that counts is the test photos."]}, {"n": 48, "part": 1, "title": "Assignment 3.1 — The Training Page, Running", "paras": ["This is Assignment three point one (3.1): the training page, running. It closes on Sunday, November fifteenth (15), at eleven fifty-nine (11:59) at night.", "1. First, src/train.py and the page, built with Claude Code.", "2. Second, the page must let you choose the data, start, and see the progress.", "3. Third, your docs/experiment.md, with the before and the after.", "4. Fourth, your requests to Claude, including the ones that didn't work.", "5. And last, a short recording or screenshots that show it running on your machine. You submit the link to your repository and your evidence. And remember: Claude writes the code, but the reason for the experiment is YOURS."]}, {"n": 49, "part": 1, "title": "Your Log, Your Commit", "paras": ["Before we finish: the log and the commit.", "1. Here's an example log. Look at what it includes: what failed, the first request that didn't work, and what was decided.", "2. So what do you write down? Your requests, what failed, and what you checked.", "3. And what has to be yours: why you chose that data, and what you learned."]}, {"n": 50, "part": 1, "title": "Class 3.1: Summary", "paras": ["We close today's class with a summary in four (4) columns.", "1. The training. It's batches and epochs, you look at the curves, and you use best.pt.", "2. Transfer learning. We start from COCO, with the same network and the same time, but checking what it assumes.", "3. Your decision. Everything, one class, or a subset, writing why first, and letting Claude question it.", "4. Your page. src/train.py and the second page, with ten (10) tests, two (2) epochs first, and then the real training.", "5. On Wednesday, in Class three point two (3.2), we're going to see what your detector really learned, and you'll meet your team of reviewers. Remember that Assignment three point one (3.1) closes on Sunday, November fifteenth (15). If you get stuck, send me an email or write in the course forum. Thank you very much for your attention, and I'll see you on Wednesday. You can disconnect now. Valentina, can you wait for me a moment while I get some water?", "Short answers in case they ask:", "- Yes, you can stay connected. Let me help Valentina in private, and then I'll continue with you. If you're in a hurry, we can also meet on Wednesday.", "- Okay, see you on Wednesday.", "- Well, I don't know how long I'll take with Valentina. You can also send me an email, and we'll set up a meeting outside class hours. Just let me know."]}, {"n": 52, "part": 2, "title": "Reading What the Detector Produces", "paras": ["Good evening, everyone, and welcome to the second class of Module three (3), Class three point two (3.2). On Monday you built the training page, and your detector started to learn. Today we read what it produced. And here's the surprise of today: a high number doesn't always mean a good detector. By the end of the class you'll see why. And I'll introduce you to your team of reviewers."]}, {"n": 53, "part": 2, "title": "Monday, in Class 3.1", "paras": ["Before we start, a quick look back at Monday.", "1. First, the training, and how the curves tell us whether it really learned.", "2. Second, the photos you train on are part of the experiment.", "3. Third, your training page, the second page of your system.", "4. And that brings us here. Your detector gives you a number. Today's question: what does that number really mean?"]}, {"n": 54, "part": 2, "title": "Today, in Three Questions", "paras": ["Today, three (3) questions.", "1. First: is this box correct? That's where IoU, precision, recall and mAP come in.", "2. Second: what does the confidence threshold decide? We'll move it on the screen, and say what each move adds and what it takes away.", "3. Third: can I trust the number? For that, we'll look at the images, with Claude reviewing.", "4. And at the end, your third Claude Code tool: subagents."]}, {"n": 55, "part": 2, "title": "Is This Box Correct?", "paras": ["Let's start with the most basic question. The detector draws a box. How do we know it's right?"]}, {"n": 56, "part": 2, "title": "What Counts as Correct?", "paras": ["Look at this photo.", "1. It's a real test photo, with two (2) boxes.", "2. The green box was drawn by a person. The red box came from the detector.", "3. They don't match exactly. And they never will. So the question isn't \"are they the same?\". The question is: how close is close enough? Until now we said \"a correct box\" as if it were obvious. It isn't."]}, {"n": 57, "part": 2, "title": "IoU: Overlap Divided by Union", "paras": ["We need a number for \"close enough\". That number is called IoU: Intersection over Union.", "1. Watch the red box. It moves toward the green one. The dark area is where the two (2) boxes overlap. Far apart, the number is low, and it's red. As they cover each other, it climbs and turns green. And when they match perfectly, it reaches one (1).", "2. So zero (0) means they don't touch at all. One (1) means they're the same box.", "3. And the math is simple. Take the area they share, and divide it by the area they cover together. Why divide? Because then size doesn't matter. A huge pothole and a tiny one are judged by the same rule. And it works the same for a pothole or a person."]}, {"n": 58, "part": 2, "title": "Three Real Boxes", "paras": ["So far, squares on a screen. Now let's try it on real boxes from our detector.", "1. First, one that's almost perfect. Easy: correct.", "2. Second, one that barely passes. Look at it. It's not a great box, and yet the metric counts it as correct.", "3. Third, here's the strange one. The detector found the pothole. It's right there. But the box is so far off that, for the metric, this is a mistake.", "4. So where is the line? The usual rule is zero point five (0.5): at that IoU or above, it counts as correct. Here's the thing: that's a convention, not a law of nature. Somebody chose it. And in a moment, you'll see how much that choice matters."]}, {"n": 59, "part": 2, "title": "Who Decided 0.5?", "paras": ["So who decided it should be zero point five (0.5)?", "1. Look at the table. Same detector, same photos. Ask for a stricter rule, and it suddenly looks much worse. But nothing about the model changed. The only thing that changed is what we decided to call \"correct\".", "2. People decided it, not math. It came from a famous competition, and it stuck. So always say which IoU you used. Without it, your number means nothing. And if someone tells you their model scored this or that, your first question should be: with what rule?"]}, {"n": 60, "part": 2, "title": "Predict First, Then Ask Claude", "paras": ["Before we go on, a way to use AI to really learn.", "1. I ask Claude to run my model on one photo, and give me the IoU of each box, with both rules. This kind of request isn't so that Claude thinks for you. It's so it does the boring part, with your own data.", "2. Here's the real answer. Two (2) boxes. With the easy rule, both count. With the strict rule, only one does. And look at the detail it points out: one single loose box counts as two (2) mistakes. A false alarm, and a missed pothole.", "3. Here's the trick. Before you ask, say out loud what you think will come out. Then let Claude do the math, on your own photos. That way it isn't Claude who learns. It's you."]}, {"n": 61, "part": 2, "title": "Three Possible Outcomes", "paras": ["We know when a box is correct. So in every photo, only three (3) things can happen.", "1. Here's a real photo, with the three (3) colors.", "2. Green: a true positive. It found a real pothole. That's what we want.", "3. Red: a false positive. It raised the alarm, and there was nothing there. It wasted someone's time. It sent a crew to a street with no potholes.", "4. Yellow: a false negative. There was a pothole, and it didn't see it. In our case, this is the dangerous one: the pothole is still there, and someone could damage their car. And with these three (3) colors, we build everything else."]}, {"n": 62, "part": 2, "title": "Precision: Can I Trust the Alarm?", "paras": ["The first measure is called precision.", "1. Look at the boxes in this photo.", "2. The question is: of everything it marked, how much was real?", "3. You calculate it as green, divided by green plus red.", "4. High precision means few false alarms. When the detector shouts \"pothole!\", you can believe it. But look at what's missing: the potholes it never marked don't count here at all. That's why precision alone isn't enough."]}, {"n": 63, "part": 2, "title": "Recall: Did We Find Them?", "paras": ["The second one is called recall.", "1. Same photo.", "2. Now a different question: of all the potholes that are really there, how many did it find?", "3. You calculate it as green, divided by green plus yellow.", "4. High recall means few potholes are missed. These are two (2) different questions, and we need both. Why? Imagine a detector that marks the whole photo as one big pothole. Perfect recall. And useless. Now imagine one that marks only one box, the one it's surest about. Perfect precision. Also useless. That's why they always go together."]}, {"n": 64, "part": 2, "title": "Count Them on One Photo", "paras": ["Let's do it once, by hand, on one photo.", "1. Count the colors with me. Green: found correctly. Red: a false alarm. Yellow: missed.", "2. Here's the table, with how many of each.", "3. And here's the interesting part. Precision looks great: almost no false alarms. But recall says it missed more potholes than it found. If you only looked at precision, you'd think this detector is excellent. And that's the whole idea, on one single photo."]}, {"n": 65, "part": 2, "title": "You Cannot Have Both", "paras": ["So can't you have both high?", "1. Look at this chart, made with the test photos. The dark line is precision. The red line is recall. Both change with the confidence threshold. They cross. And the dotted line is the value Ultralytics uses by default.", "2. Ask for more confidence: fewer false alarms, but more potholes missed. Ask for less: it finds more, but it makes more mistakes.", "3. So the threshold slides between the two (2). Nothing comes for free. Later, we'll move it on the screen."]}, {"n": 66, "part": 2, "title": "Claude Builds the Table for You", "paras": ["Now, will you count all this by hand, on a hundred (100) photos? Of course not. That's what Claude is for.", "1. This is the real output of evaluate.py, this week's module. One row for each threshold.", "2. Claude builds it from the specification, with ten (10) tests, and your notebook uses it with your own model. Claude wrote the function. The tests came in the kit. That's why you can trust that it counts correctly.", "3. That's the idea: you understood the concept on one photo. The hundred (100) photos are Claude's job."]}, {"n": 67, "part": 2, "title": "From Two Numbers to One: AP", "paras": ["But often we want one single number. How do we get one?", "1. Each threshold gives us one point: one precision, one recall. Join all the points, and you get this curve.", "2. The area under the curve is called Average Precision. A good detector stays high for longer, so its area is bigger. And mAP is that same number, averaged over all the classes. You don't need to calculate the area by hand. What matters is the idea: one number that sums up the whole curve, without choosing any threshold."]}, {"n": 68, "part": 2, "title": "mAP50 and mAP50-95", "paras": ["And this is where the two (2) numbers the assignment asks for come from.", "1. mAP fifty (mAP50) counts a box as correct when the IoU is zero point five (0.5) or more. Here: zero point eight two (0.82).", "2. mAP fifty to ninety-five (mAP50-95) averages many rules, from the easy one to a very strict one. Here it drops to zero point five three (0.53).", "3. Same detector. So what is it telling us? It finds the potholes, but its boxes aren't always exact. And that's already an interpretation, not just a number. That's what the assignment asks you for. Copying the number isn't enough. You have to say what it means."]}, {"n": 69, "part": 2, "title": "What mAP Hides", "paras": ["And now the most important part of this section. What does mAP hide?", "1. Here's our sign detector, class by class.", "2. The average is zero point nine one (0.91). Excellent, right? But look at the bars. STOP and speed limit: almost perfect. The traffic light: far behind. Report only the average, and you'd never see it. So with several classes, always look class by class. And if you only have one class, like our potholes, the size of the objects still matters."]}, {"n": 70, "part": 2, "title": "mAP Is Not “at a Threshold”", "paras": ["And here's a mistake I'll be looking for in your assignments.", "1. This sentence sounds right, and it's wrong: \"at a confidence of zero point five (0.5), the mAP is zero point eight two (0.82), so it finds eighty-two percent (82%) of the potholes.\" Two (2) mistakes. First, mAP has no threshold. Second, mAP doesn't tell you how many potholes it finds. Recall does.", "2. Here's the correct version. It says which number is which, and which IoU was used.", "3. Why? Because mAP already goes through all the thresholds. To talk about one threshold, you use precision and recall."]}, {"n": 71, "part": 2, "title": "What Does Your Number Hide?", "paras": ["And Claude can help us here too.", "1. I gave it the results per class, and asked: what does the average hide?", "2. Here's the answer. It points me straight to the traffic light, and tells me the other three (3) classes alone would score much higher. And it tells me what the file can't say: whether the problem is missed signs or false alarms. It shows me where to look. It doesn't write my conclusion."]}, {"n": 72, "part": 2, "title": "Can Claude See?", "paras": ["Before the break, an experiment I'd wanted to try for days. Claude can open images. So... can it see potholes?"]}, {"n": 73, "part": 2, "title": "Claude Against Our Detector", "paras": ["I really tried it.", "1. Here's the request, the same for every photo: open the image, find every pothole, and give me the boxes. That's it. No code. No model to download.", "2. No training, no dataset, no labels. Just the question. Then I scored its boxes exactly like YOLO's: the same photos, the same code. Before you see the next slide: who do you think wins?"]}, {"n": 74, "part": 2, "title": "The Result", "paras": ["Here's the result.", "1. Look at the table.", "2. They found exactly the same number of potholes, and Claude gave fewer false alarms, without any training. I didn't expect that. But look at the last column. YOLO takes a fraction of a second. Claude takes about ten (10). And careful: it's only thirty (30) photos, and in one of them Claude marked seven (7) extra boxes. For a camera on a moving car, ten (10) seconds per photo is useless."]}, {"n": 75, "part": 2, "title": "So Why Train a Detector at All?", "paras": ["And that raises an uncomfortable question. Why train a detector at all?", "1. A trained detector is fast. It works with no internet, on your own machine, and you can measure it. And one more thing: it always gives the same kind of answer, so you can measure it on a hundred (100) photos or on a million.", "2. Asking Claude needs no training and no data. It can explain what it sees, and it's great for trying an idea or reviewing mistakes. But it's slower, and it needs a connection. Imagine checking the photos of a whole city with Claude. It would take forever, and every car would need internet.", "3. So they don't compete. Train the detector for the road, and use Claude to review what it does. That combination is what we want you to learn."]}, {"n": 76, "part": 2, "title": "Break 2 · 5 Minutes", "paras": ["Perfect. You now know what a correct box is, what precision, recall and mAP measure, and even what happens when we ask Claude.", "1. Before the second part, a five (5) minute break, like on Monday. Get up, walk, stretch, drink some water. The second part is worth it, I promise.", "Welcome back. Let's continue with the threshold, and then your team of reviewers."]}, {"n": 77, "part": 2, "title": "Move the Threshold, Watch the Boxes", "paras": ["Now let's move the threshold on the screen, on the same photo. Watch what appears, and what disappears.", "1. A very low threshold. It finds every pothole. But look at all those red boxes: false alarms. A low threshold tells the detector: mark everything that might be a pothole, even if you're not sure.", "2. Now we raise it to the normal value, the one Ultralytics comes with. Most of the red boxes are gone. But one pothole already got away, in yellow.", "3. And a very high threshold. No red boxes at all. Perfect, right? Look again. It missed every single pothole. Zero (0) mistakes, and zero potholes found. That's why high precision, alone, means nothing."]}, {"n": 78, "part": 2, "title": "What Each Move Adds and Removes", "paras": ["Now the same thing, on all the test photos.", "1. Here's the table. As the threshold goes up, precision goes up, and recall goes down.", "2. Going from zero point two five (0.25) to zero point five (0.50) removes many false alarms. But it also loses real potholes. Every move has a price, and your job is to say that price out loud. That's exactly what the assignment asks for. And there's no correct value in this table. It depends on who uses your detector."]}, {"n": 79, "part": 2, "title": "What Do I Win, What Do I Lose?", "paras": ["So how do we decide? I asked Claude for help, but I gave it the context first.", "1. I tell it who will use the system: a city crew that decides which potholes to repair. This matters a lot. Without that context, you get a general answer. With it, you get a useful one.", "2. And here's the real answer. It tells me what I win and what I lose. Then it says it would start at zero point two five (0.25), if the crew checks the photos before sending a truck. Why? Because a false alarm costs a look, and a missed pothole stays a danger.", "3. And look at how it ends: ask the crew how much each mistake costs them. The number alone doesn't decide. That's the right question."]}, {"n": 80, "part": 2, "title": "Who Pays for Each Mistake?", "paras": ["Let's look at three (3) examples.", "1. Look at the table. For the pothole report, you'd choose a low threshold, because a missed pothole is worse. For a camera that stops a machine, low too, because someone could get hurt. For tagging photos in an app, high, because a wrong tag is annoying and nobody notices a missing one. Same detector. The right column changes with the problem.", "2. So the threshold isn't a technical detail. It's a decision about who pays for each mistake."]}, {"n": 81, "part": 2, "title": "Choose Yours, and Say Why", "paras": ["So here's what I'll ask you in the assignment.", "1. These three (3) sentences, with your own words and your own numbers. Just three (3) sentences. If you can fill in the blanks, you understood this class.", "2. Claude can give you the numbers. But the \"because\" has to be YOURS."]}, {"n": 82, "part": 2, "title": "Look at the Detections", "paras": ["So far we've talked about numbers. Now let's do what almost nobody does: look at the images."]}, {"n": 83, "part": 2, "title": "Three That Are Right", "paras": ["First, three (3) that are right.", "1. The first one. A green box on each pothole. Nothing red, nothing yellow.", "2. The second one.", "3. The third one.", "4. What do they have in common? Large objects, close to the camera, easy to see. That's where our detector does well. If your users always took photos like these, this model would be excellent."]}, {"n": 84, "part": 2, "title": "Three That Are Wrong", "paras": ["Now, three (3) that are wrong.", "1. The first one. Look at all the yellow: potholes it missed. It found the large ones, and missed the small ones.", "2. The second one: more of the same, especially far down the road.", "3. The third one: even false alarms, right next to real potholes.", "4. Far, small, and in groups. Remember the box sizes you counted in Module two (2)? Here they are. mAP doesn't tell you this. You only see it by looking. And this is where the improvement comes from. Not a bigger model. More photos with far, well-labeled potholes. Or a larger image size."]}, {"n": 85, "part": 2, "title": "The Metric Says Yes, You Say No", "paras": ["And now a very interesting case.", "1. Look at this photo.", "2. For the metric, this box is correct. It passes the rule easily.", "3. But look closely. It includes part of the shadow, and it leaves out a piece of the pothole. Would you send a crew based on that? This is exactly what the activity asks for: find a detection the metric accepts, and you wouldn't. And it depends on what your user needs. To count potholes, this box works. To measure the size of the repair, it doesn't."]}, {"n": 86, "part": 2, "title": "Claude Looks at the Photos", "paras": ["And who can help you look? Claude can do this too, because it really opens images.", "1. This is the figure your notebook produces, with three (3) correct and three (3) wrong.", "2. And this is the real report from a subagent called image-auditor. It grouped the mistakes by type: most of them are small, far objects. And something I liked a lot: it says what it can't know. At this resolution, it can't see what's inside some boxes. It ends with a question. I checked those mistakes by opening the photos, and yes, most are what it says. But it can be wrong too. So check it yourself.", "3. It opens the images, groups the mistakes, and says what it doesn't know. The conclusion is still yours."]}, {"n": 87, "part": 2, "title": "When the Metric and the Images Disagree", "paras": ["So what do we do when the metric and the images disagree?", "1. Believe the images. Then find out why. Because a metric is only as good as the labels behind it.", "2. First, a \"false alarm\" may be right. If there's a real pothole that nobody labeled, the model gets punished for finding it. This happens more than you'd think. Open the photo. Sometimes the model saw a pothole the person labeling didn't.", "3. Second, a \"correct\" box may be useless. What your user needs decides, not the IoU.", "4. That's why the assignment asks: what would you change about the data, not the model? That's almost always where the improvement is."]}, {"n": 88, "part": 2, "title": "Your Review Team: Subagents", "paras": ["You just saw image-auditor at work. Now let's look at what it is, and how it's made. It's your third Claude Code tool: subagents."]}, {"n": 89, "part": 2, "title": "What Is a Subagent?", "paras": ["So what's a subagent?", "1. It's a separate Claude, with its own instructions. It looks at your work with fresh eyes, because it didn't do the work. And here's the key difference with a Skill: the subagent doesn't see your conversation. It starts clean.", "2. And here's where we are on the map. Module one (1): CLAUDE.md. Module two (2): Skills. Today: subagents. Next week: hooks.", "3. Why a separate one? Because whoever wrote the code is the worst person to review it. That's true for us, and it's true for Claude. It's like asking a colleague to read your report."]}, {"n": 90, "part": 2, "title": "A Subagent Is a File", "paras": ["And the best part: a subagent is nothing mysterious. It's a file.", "1. This one comes in your kit. It's called metrics-reviewer.", "2. At the top, its name and description. Claude reads them to know when to use it.", "3. Then, its tools. And look: it can only read. It can't change your files, even if it wanted to. That's a great protection. A reviewer shouldn't be able to change what it's reviewing.", "4. And below, its instructions: exactly what to check. These are the mistakes we saw today. And since it's just a text file, you can change it. If your project has another typical mistake, add it to the list."]}, {"n": 91, "part": 2, "title": "Skill or Subagent?", "paras": ["So how is it different from a Skill?", "1. Look at the table. A Skill works inside your conversation. A subagent works on its own. A Skill knows everything you've talked about. A subagent knows only what you ask it to read. That's why explain-run is a Skill, and metrics-reviewer is a subagent. One does a task for you. The other asks you questions about what you did.", "2. So: want something done? Use a Skill. Want it questioned? Use a subagent."]}, {"n": 92, "part": 2, "title": "A Bad Draft, on Purpose", "paras": ["Let's put it to the test. I wrote a bad interpretation, on purpose.", "1. Read it with me. It says the detector is very good, and it uses real numbers.", "2. Sounds good, right? Before the next slide: how many problems can you find? Take a moment."]}, {"n": 93, "part": 2, "title": "What the Reviewer Found", "paras": ["And here's the real report from metrics-reviewer.", "1. It found that talking about mAP at a threshold is wrong. That I confused mAP with recall. That I used training photos as evidence. That precision can't see the missed potholes. And that my improvement only talks about the model, not the data. And it uses the numbers in my own file to show me each mistake.", "2. Five (5) problems, each one with its line and its number. And the most important part: it didn't rewrite my text. It only asked me questions. Fixing it is my job."]}, {"n": 94, "part": 2, "title": "Check Your Reviewer Too", "paras": ["And like everything else, you check your reviewer too.", "1. First, make sure it's there: type /agents in Claude Code.", "2. Second, give it a text with mistakes on purpose. If it doesn't find them, its instructions aren't good enough. It's like what we did with the CLAUDE.md rules last week.", "3. Third, check with git status that it didn't touch anything.", "4. And last, remember that it can be wrong. It's another model, reading what you show it. You have the last word."]}, {"n": 95, "part": 2, "title": "Activity, Part 2: Move the Threshold", "paras": ["Now your turn. This is the second part of the activity.", "1. First, build src/evaluate.py with Claude. Plan, approval, and tests: fifty-seven (57) in total must pass.", "2. Second, run the notebook zero three my results (03_my_results), with the model you trained.", "3. Third, say what each threshold adds and removes, and choose one.", "4. Fourth, ask image-auditor about your mistakes, and find a \"correct\" box that you wouldn't accept.", "5. And last, write your draft, and ask metrics-reviewer to question it. IMPORTANT: with this, you already have almost all of Assignment three point two (3.2)."]}, {"n": 96, "part": 2, "title": "Your Week", "paras": ["That's the concept part. What's left is for your week: the most common mistakes, Assignment three point two (3.2), and how to close your experiment."]}, {"n": 97, "part": 2, "title": "Common Mistakes and How to Spot Them", "paras": ["Before the assignment, four (4) mistakes to avoid.", "1. First, talking about mAP at a threshold. At a threshold, you report precision and recall.", "2. Second, measuring with training photos. Use the test photos.", "3. Third, saying the only improvement is \"more epochs\". The assignment asks about the data.", "4. And fourth, believing a false alarm without opening the photo. The label might be missing."]}, {"n": 98, "part": 2, "title": "Assignment 3.2 — Results and Their Interpretation", "paras": ["This is Assignment three point two (3.2): the results, and what they mean. It closes on Sunday, November fifteenth (15), at eleven fifty-nine (11:59) at night, the same day as Assignment three point one (3.1).", "1. First, src/evaluate.py, built with Claude, with fifty-seven (57) tests in total.", "2. Second, mAP fifty (mAP50) and mAP fifty to ninety-five (mAP50-95), plus precision and recall at two (2) or more thresholds, saying which IoU you used.", "3. Third, three (3) correct detections and three (3) wrong ones, with the images.", "4. Fourth, what it learned, what it confuses, and whether the metric matches what you see.", "5. And last, what you would change about the data, not the model. You submit the link to your repository and your report. And remember: the subagents question it, but the interpretation is YOURS."]}, {"n": 99, "part": 2, "title": "Close Your Experiment", "paras": ["And don't forget to close your experiment.", "1. It's the part of docs/experiment.md you left empty on Monday: what really happened, whether you were right, and what you learned.", "2. And being wrong here isn't a bad grade. A wrong prediction, well explained, shows you really ran an experiment."]}, {"n": 100, "part": 2, "title": "Your Log, Your Commit", "paras": ["One last thing: the log and the commit.", "1. Here's an example log. Look at the last two (2) lines: what the auditor said, and that I checked it; and the mistake the reviewer found, which I fixed myself.", "2. So what do you write down? Your requests, what you checked, and what you fixed.", "3. And what has to be yours: the threshold, the interpretation, and the idea about the data."]}, {"n": 101, "part": 2, "title": "Module 3: Summary", "paras": ["Let's close with the summary of the module, in four (4) columns.", "1. Is it correct? IoU, always saying which one you used, plus true positives, false positives and false negatives.", "2. The numbers. Precision tells you if you can believe the alarm. Recall tells you if it found them all. And mAP can hide what happens in each class.", "3. The threshold. Every move has a price, and you decide who pays it.", "4. Your team. evaluate.py with its tests, image-auditor who looks, and metrics-reviewer who questions.", "5. Next week we finish the system: the detection page, and your last Claude tool, hooks. Remember that both assignments close on Sunday, November fifteenth (15). If you get stuck, send me an email. Thank you very much for your attention, and I'll see you in Module four (4)."]}];
